---
import OrcaDocs from "../../../../components/OrcaDocs.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import { ORCA_REPO } from "../../../../data/orca";

const DOCS = `${ORCA_REPO}/blob/main/docs`;

const description =
  "Worker pools in Orca: workers that claim a pool's runs over HTTPS, pools that Orca grows and shrinks itself on Hetzner or EC2, what counts as demand, how a machine boots and is removed, out-of-stock and quota fallback, the private network, and one run spread over several machines with a dista cluster of its own.";
---

<OrcaDocs href="/s/orca/docs/pools" description={description}>
  <p class="eyebrow">Operations</p>
  <h1 class="section-title">Pools and autoscaling</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A pool is a set of machines that run the runs of the pipelines placed on it. You can
    start its workers by hand, or let Orca make and remove machines as runs wait. One run
    can also spread its dista cluster over several machines of a pool.
  </p>

  <nav class="docs-toc" aria-label="On this page">
    <a href="#worker-pools">Worker pools</a>
    <a href="#autoscaling">Autoscaling</a>
    <a href="#demand">Demand</a>
    <a href="#making-a-machine">Making a machine</a>
    <a href="#removing-machines">Removing machines</a>
    <a href="#hetzner">Hetzner</a>
    <a href="#ec2">EC2</a>
    <a href="#one-run-many-machines">One run, several machines</a>
    <a href="#in-the-ui">In the UI</a>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="worker-pools">Worker pools</h2>
  <p>
    A pipeline registered with <code>placement = "worker:&lt;pool&gt;"</code> is never
    started by the scheduler. Its runs stay queued until a worker of that pool claims one
    over the UI's HTTPS API. The worker runs the pipeline's binary on its own machine and
    records the run's state through the same API, so it never needs database credentials
    or an inbound port. A pool's name is 1 to 32 of <code>a-z</code>, <code>0-9</code>,
    <code>-</code> and <code>_</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/s/orca/docs/workers">Remote workers</a> covers a worker started by hand:
    its token, the container, fills on a pool and what happens when a worker dies. The
    rest of this page is about pools that Orca runs for you.
  </p>

  <h2 id="autoscaling">Autoscaling</h2>
  <p>
    Since 0.23.0, a <code>[pools.&lt;name&gt;]</code> table in orca.toml makes a pool
    that Orca grows and shrinks itself. Pipelines run on it with
    <code>placement = "worker:&lt;name&gt;"</code>, as on workers started by hand.
    <code>orca register</code> records the pool in <code>orca.pools</code>, and the
    leading scheduler looks at every pool every <code>ORCA_POOL_SCALE_SECONDS</code>
    (15).
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="orca.toml" code={`[pools.lab]
provider = "hetzner"
machines = [
  { type = "cax31", wait = 600 },   # prefer this; keep trying it for 10 min
  { type = "cax21", wait = 600 },
  { type = "cpx32" },               # the last: no wait, keep trying it
]
locations = ["hel1", "nbg1", "fsn1"]
max_machines = 3
idle_seconds = 900

[pipelines.history]
placement = "worker:lab"
memory_gb = "auto"`} />
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>What it sets</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>provider</code></td><td>where machines are made: <code>"hetzner"</code> or <code>"ec2"</code></td><td class="ref">required</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>machines</code></td><td>machine types in order of preference, each with a <code>wait</code> in seconds; every entry but the last needs one</td><td class="ref">required</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>locations</code></td><td>where to try each type, in order: Hetzner locations (required there), or EC2 subnet IDs</td><td class="ref">none</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>max_machines</code></td><td>the most machines the pool has at once, 1 to 100</td><td class="ref">1</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>idle_seconds</code></td><td>how long a machine may sit idle, with nothing waiting, before it's removed</td><td class="ref">900</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    <a href="/s/orca/docs/config">orca.toml</a> has every key and its limits. A pool the
    file no longer has is deleted when you register again, and its machines are removed
    once they are idle.
  </p>

  <h3 id="machine-types">Machine types and waits</h3>
  <p>
    The <code>machines</code> list is a fallback order. Orca tries the type it's on in
    each location in turn, and the first location that has it gets the machine. If no
    location has it, Orca keeps trying the same type, at most once every
    <code>ORCA_POOL_TRY_SECONDS</code> (60), until that type's <code>wait</code> has
    passed. Then it moves to the next type. The last type has no wait and is tried for as
    long as there is demand. Once a machine is made, or the demand goes away, Orca starts
    again from the first type. A machine that is running is never replaced by a type
    higher in the list: the order only matters when a machine is added.
  </p>
  <p>
    Orca also skips a type whose CPU architecture the worker image has no variant for. It
    reads the image's manifest from the registry (an OCI index or a Docker manifest list,
    every <code>ORCA_POOL_IMAGE_SECONDS</code>, 600) and compares its architectures with
    the one the provider gives for the type. An arm64-only image never gets an x86
    machine, and the pool's status says the type was skipped.
  </p>

  <h3 id="out-of-stock">Out of stock and quota</h3>
  <p>
    A provider can refuse a type for two different reasons, and Orca treats them
    differently:
  </p>
  <ul class="docs-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Out of stock.</strong> Hetzner's <code>resource_unavailable</code>,
      <code>placement_error</code> or <code>server_type_unavailable</code>, or EC2's
      <code>InsufficientInstanceCapacity</code>, <code>InsufficientCapacity</code> or
      <code>Unsupported</code>, means "not here, not now". Orca tries the next location,
      and keeps trying the type for its <code>wait</code>, as above.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Quota</strong> (from 0.24.0). Hetzner's
      <code>resource_limit_exceeded</code>, or EC2's <code>VcpuLimitExceeded</code> or
      <code>InstanceLimitExceeded</code>, means the project's limit is reached. Waiting
      doesn't free quota, and every location shares it, so Orca moves on at once to the
      next usable type in <code>machines</code>, in the same pass, since a smaller type
      may still fit. When none fits, the pool's status says
      <code>can't grow: the project's quota fits none of its types</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Anything else</strong> is reported in the pool's status and tried again
      later.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <code>orca.pools.status</code> always says what the last try came to.
  </p>

  <h2 id="demand">Demand</h2>
  <p>
    A pool needs another machine when its pipelines (not paused) have runs that have
    waited <code>ORCA_POOL_GROW_AFTER</code> seconds (20) and that a worker could start.
    That counts no more of a pipeline's runs than its <code>max_runs</code> less those
    running, plus its fill runs. Workers already in the pool take what they have room for
    first, and what is left waiting is the demand. A running run that wants more cluster
    members than have joined it (<a href="#one-run-many-machines">below</a>, from
    0.24.0) counts as demand too.
  </p>
  <p>
    While there is demand, no machine of the pool is still booting, and the pool has
    fewer than <code>max_machines</code>, Orca makes one machine. So a pool grows one
    machine at a time, each once the one before it has joined.
  </p>

  <h2 id="making-a-machine">Making a machine</h2>
  <p>
    Each machine is named <code>orca-pool-&lt;pool&gt;-&lt;n&gt;</code> (from 0.24.0;
    <code>orca-&lt;pool&gt;-&lt;n&gt;</code> before) and labelled with the pool and this
    stack's id, so several stacks can share one cloud project and keep their machines
    apart. Orca creates a worker token for the machine alone and puts it in the machine's
    startup script, which holds no other secret. When the machine boots, the script:
  </p>
  <ol class="docs-list">
    <li>
      asks the UI's <code>/api/worker/boot</code> with that token, over TLS, for the rest:
      the worker image (<code>ORCA_WORKER_IMAGE</code>, the server's own), the login to
      pull it, and the bucket's variables (<code>ORCA_WAREHOUSE</code>,
      <code>ORCA_LOGS</code> and the <code>AWS_*</code> ones from the UI's environment);
    </li>
    <li>
      finds its private address in the provider's metadata, for runs spread over several
      machines (from 0.24.0);
    </li>
    <li>
      installs Docker if it isn't there, pulls the image (Docker picks the variant for the
      machine's architecture from the same manifest), and runs <code>orca worker --pool
      &lt;pool&gt; --name &lt;machine&gt; --max-runs 64</code> with host networking and
      run cgroups.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <code>/api/worker/boot</code> answers only a token the autoscaler made for a machine
    that is live, so a token from <code>orca worker-token create</code> gets nothing from
    it. The worker takes as many runs as its own host can
    <a href="/s/orca/docs/scaling#vertical">grant</a>, up to 64.
  </p>

  <h2 id="removing-machines">Removing machines</h2>
  <p>
    To remove a machine, Orca revokes its token, deletes the machine, and ends its row in
    <code>orca.pool_machines</code> saying why. That happens when:
  </p>
  <ul class="docs-list">
    <li>it has run nothing for the pool's <code>idle_seconds</code> and the pool has no demand;</li>
    <li>its pool is no longer in orca.toml, once it is idle;</li>
    <li>
      its worker never showed up within <code>ORCA_POOL_BOOT_SECONDS</code> (900), or went
      silent for <code>ORCA_POOL_LOST_SECONDS</code> (300). Its runs are then queued again,
      as any lost worker's are;
    </li>
    <li>
      it carries the pool's and this stack's labels but Orca has no live row for it, such
      as a machine made just before the scheduler stopped.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A machine that runs only a cluster member of another machine's run is busy, not idle.
  </p>

  <h2 id="hetzner">Hetzner</h2>
  <p>
    The scheduler needs a read-write Hetzner API token for the project the machines go
    in: <code>ORCA_HCLOUD_TOKEN</code>, or <code>ORCA_HCLOUD_TOKEN_SECRET</code> naming
    the secret that holds it. Each machine gets the firewall and SSH keys named in
    <code>ORCA_HCLOUD_FIREWALL</code> and <code>ORCA_HCLOUD_SSH_KEYS</code>, and the OS
    image <code>ORCA_HCLOUD_IMAGE</code> (<code>ubuntu-24.04</code>).
  </p>
  <p>
    From 0.24.0, every machine joins a private network, <code>orca-&lt;stack id&gt;</code>, which Orca
    makes in <code>eu-central</code> over <code>10.77.0.0/16</code> if it isn't there
    (<code>ORCA_HCLOUD_NETWORK</code>, <code>ORCA_HCLOUD_NETWORK_ZONE</code> and
    <code>ORCA_HCLOUD_NETWORK_RANGE</code> change it; <code>none</code> for none). Its
    worker advertises its address on that network, so a run spread over several machines
    talks over it.
  </p>
  <p>
    On the Hetzner stack, <code>HCLOUD_POOL_TOKEN_FILE</code> in
    <code>hetzner.env</code> (or a key in <code>SECRETS_FILE</code>) is all you set:
    <code>deploy.sh</code> sends the token to the server and fills in the rest, with the
    stack's own firewall (ssh from <code>ADMIN_IP</code> only) and SSH key
    (<a href={`${DOCS}/hetzner.md#worker-pools-that-grow-themselves`} rel="noopener">hetzner.md</a>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="ec2">EC2</h2>
  <p>
    With <code>provider = "ec2"</code>, the scheduler uses <code>AWS_REGION</code> and its
    credentials or instance role. A pool's <code>locations</code> are subnet IDs, tried
    in order; with none, EC2's default subnet or <code>ORCA_EC2_SUBNET</code>. Each
    instance gets:
  </p>
  <ul class="docs-list">
    <li>
      the security group <code>ORCA_POOL_EC2_SECURITY_GROUP</code> and instance profile
      <code>ORCA_POOL_EC2_PROFILE</code> (else <code>ORCA_EC2_SECURITY_GROUP</code> and
      <code>ORCA_EC2_PROFILE</code>). The profile's role is how its worker reaches the
      bucket;
    </li>
    <li>
      Ubuntu 24.04 for the type's architecture, from Canonical's public SSM parameter, or
      the image in <code>ORCA_POOL_EC2_AMI_ARM64</code> or
      <code>ORCA_POOL_EC2_AMI_AMD64</code>;
    </li>
    <li>
      IMDSv2 only, terminate on shutdown, and the tags <code>orca=pool</code>,
      <code>orca-pool=&lt;pool&gt;</code>, <code>orca-owner=&lt;stack&gt;</code> and
      <code>Name</code>. Its worker advertises the instance's private address.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="one-run-many-machines">One run over several machines</h2>
  <p>
    From 0.24.0, a pipeline with dista tasks can spread one run's cluster over several
    workers of its pool. <code>workers = N</code> (or <code>orca register --workers N</code>) on a
    pipeline placed <code>worker:&lt;pool&gt;</code> lets each run use up to N more
    machines than the one that starts it. Without it, a run stays on one machine.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="orca.toml" code={`[pipelines.fanout]
placement = "worker:lab"
workers = 2      # up to 2 more machines join each run`} />
  <ul class="docs-list">
    <li>
      <strong>The head.</strong> The worker that claims the run starts it as a cluster of
      its own: a dista head on a free port, a dista worker, and the run's driver. It
      reports the head's address to the UI. That address is the one the worker was
      started with, <code>--advertise &lt;address&gt;</code> or
      <code>ORCA_WORKER_ADVERTISE</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Members.</strong> Another worker of the pool that has room and nothing to
      start is given the run to join. It starts a dista worker against the head, sized as
      a run of the pipeline would be on its machine and counted against its room. dista
      puts a worker that joins a running job to work at once.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The cluster token.</strong> Each run start gets a token of its own, given as
      <code>DISTA_TOKEN</code> to the head, the driver and every member, so nothing else
      on the network can join the run (<a href="/s/orca/docs/security#cluster-token">Tokens
      and secrets</a>).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The end.</strong> When the run ends, each member is told to stop on its
      worker's next claim, and a member's dista worker gives up on a head that is gone
      within 10 seconds anyway. <code>orca.run_members</code> keeps who joined each run
      start, with its grant, and when and why it ended.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A worker without <code>--advertise</code> neither starts a run as a cluster nor joins
    one, so its runs run on its machine alone. On a pool Orca grows, all of this is set
    up for you: each machine advertises its private address, its worker uses host
    networking so dista's ports are on that address, and a run that wants more members
    grows the pool, up to <code>max_machines</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="in-the-ui">In the UI</h2>
  <ul class="docs-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Ops › Pools</strong> (<code>/ops/pools?kind=self-hosted</code>,
      <code>/api/pools</code>) has a row per worker's machine, autoscaled ones included:
      its state, image, CPUs, memory, runs and last heartbeat.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Autoscaled pools</strong> (from 0.24.0). The same page has a card with a row
      per <code>[pools.&lt;name&gt;]</code>: its provider, its types with their waits, its
      locations, live machines against <code>max_machines</code>, its
      <code>idle_seconds</code>, the type it is trying and what the last try came to.
      <code>/api/pools</code> has them as <code>autoscaled</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A run's Cluster card</strong> (from 0.24.0). A run spread over several
      workers lists its head and each member: the worker, the run start, its address, its
      grant, when it joined and ended, and why. The run's JSON has the same rows.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <a href={`${DOCS}/workers.md#one-run-over-several-workers`} rel="noopener">workers.md</a>
    and <a href={`${DOCS}/orca-toml.md#worker-pools`} rel="noopener">orca-toml.md</a> in
    the Orca repository have the full detail and every variable.
  </p>
</OrcaDocs>
